{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_1099445187#11_1968694095","relevance":3,"reason":"This is a segment of the Pew Research Center article dedicated to religious groups' views on end-of-life issues. It directly discusses Christian/Episcopal opposition to active euthanasia and is part of a comparative overview that includes Buddhism, Hinduism, Islam, Judaism, and multiple Western Christian groups.","learned":"Pew reports that the Episcopal Church opposes assisted suicide and active euthanasia as morally wrong because it involves taking human life, while the full article compares this with Eastern traditions such as Buddhism and Hinduism."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3205","reason":"This query is retrieving high-quality comparative religious evidence from Pew/BBC; continue to look for additional non-duplicate segments that may include Buddhist or other Eastern views alongside Western Christian views."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3205"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_1099445187#10_1968692164","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3205","rank":4,"snippet":"Religious Groups’ Views on End-of-Life Issues November 21, 2013 Religious Groups’ Views on End-of-Life Issues Assemblies of God<cover>Buddhism Catholicism Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints (Mormon Church) Evangelical Lutheran Church in America Episcopal Church Hinduism Islam Judaism National Baptist Convention Presbyterian Church U.S.A. Seventh-day Adventist Church Southern Baptist Convention United Church of Christ Unitarian Universalist Association United Methodist Church \" , \"segment\": \"At the same time, Willer says, the church teaches that there is no requirement to take extraordinary steps to keep a dying person alive a little longer. “ Allowing something to happen is different than actively hastening death,” Willer says. For more information: ELCA Message. 1992. “ End of Life Decisions. ” ( PDF) Episcopal Church In 1991, the Episcopal Church passed a resolution against assisted suicide and other forms of active euthanasia, stating that it is “morally wrong and unacceptable to take a human life in order to relieve the suffering caused by incurable illness.” According to Timothy Sedgwick, a professor of Christian ethics </cover>at Virginia Theological Seminary, this teaching comes from the church’s broader view “that one should never take a life, even your own.” "}}
