{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_489123501#10_1051307018","relevance":3,"reason":"This is a substantive segment of the dedicated 'Religious Views on Euthanasia' page, directly discussing Jewish variation on passive/voluntary euthanasia and beginning a Shinto/Japan section, within a page comparing Eastern and Western religious traditions.","learned":"Jewish views vary by movement and religiosity, with Orthodox/Hasidic respondents more disapproving and secularized Orthodox respondents more approving; Shinto is presented as relevant to Japanese/Eastern perspectives."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3211","reason":"The query is still yielding highly relevant substantive segments from a dedicated comparative religious-views page; continue to capture additional Eastern-view evidence such as Shinto/Japan and Jainism if available."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3211"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_489123501#11_1051308978","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3211","rank":7,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_489123501#11_1051308978\" , \"end_char\": 8185.000000 , \"headings\": \"Religious Views on Euthanasia Religious Views on Euthanasia Page Contents Buddhism Christianity Catholicism Protestantism Christians in support of <cover>euthanasia Hinduism Islam Jainism Judaism Shinto Unitarian Universalism Influence of religious views</cover>See also Adapted from Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Share this: Like this: Search Quote of the Day \" , \"segment\": \"Shinto In Japan, where the dominant religion is Shinto, 69% of the religious organisations agree with the act of voluntary passive euthanasia. The corresponding figure was 75% when the family asked for it. In Shinto, the prolongation of life using artificial means is a disgraceful act against life. Views on active euthanasia are mixed, with 25% Shinto and Buddhist organisations in Japan supporting voluntary active euthanasia. Unitarian Universalism The Unitarian Universalist Association (UUA) recommends observing the ethics and culture of the resident country when determining euthanasia. In 1988 the UUA gathered to share a commitment to The Right to Die with Dignity document which included a resolution supporting self-determination in dying. Influence of religious "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"27/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_489123501#11_1051308978","relevance":3,"reason":"This is a substantive segment of the dedicated 'Religious Views on Euthanasia' page. It directly provides Eastern/Japanese evidence, reporting Shinto organizational support levels for voluntary passive euthanasia and mixed support for active euthanasia, and contrasts with Unitarian Universalist support for self-determination in dying.","learned":"In Japan/Shinto, many religious organizations agree with voluntary passive euthanasia, especially when family requests it, while support for voluntary active euthanasia is much lower and mixed; Unitarian Universalists emphasize self-determination and local cultural ethics."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3211","reason":"The current query continues to yield directly relevant segments from a dedicated comparative religious-views page; continue to see if more substantive East-West evidence remains, especially Jainism, Islam, or influence-of-religion sections."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3211"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_489123501#1_1051290505","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3211","rank":8,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_489123501#1_1051290505\" , \"end_char\": 2193.000000 , \"headings\": \"Religious Views on Euthanasia Religious Views on Euthanasia Page Contents Buddhism Christianity Catholicism Protestantism Christians in support of <cover>euthanasia Hinduism Islam Jainism Judaism Shinto Unitarian Universalism Influence of religious views</cover>See also Adapted from Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Share this: Like this: Search Quote of the Day \" , \"segment\": \"In Theravada Buddhism a lay person daily recites the simple formula: “ I undertake the precept to abstain from destroying living beings.” For Buddhist monastics ( bhikkhu) however the rules are more explicitly spelled out. For example, in the monastic code ( Patimokkha ), it states: “Should any bhikkhu intentionally deprive a human being of life, or search for an assassin for him, or praise the advantages of death, or incite him to die (thus): ‘ My good man, what use is this wretched, miserable life to you? Death would be better for you than life,’ or with such an idea in mind, such a purpose in mind, should in various ways praise the advantages of death or incite him "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"28/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_489123501#1_1051290505","relevance":3,"reason":"This is a substantive segment of the dedicated 'Religious Views on Euthanasia' page. It directly provides Eastern/Buddhist evidence, quoting Theravada precepts and monastic rules against intentionally taking life or encouraging death, within a broader East-West religious comparison.","learned":"Theravada Buddhist ethics generally oppose euthanasia by emphasizing the precept against destroying living beings and monastic rules against intentionally depriving a human being of life or inciting death."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3211","reason":"The current religious-views query is still yielding substantive grade-3 segments from a document dedicated to East-West/religious variation in euthanasia views; continue while it remains productive."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3211"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_489123501#5_1051298527","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3211","rank":9,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_489123501#5_1051298527\" , \"end_char\": 4709.000000 , \"headings\": \"Religious Views on Euthanasia Religious Views on Euthanasia Page Contents Buddhism Christianity Catholicism Protestantism Christians in support of <cover>euthanasia Hinduism Islam Jainism Judaism Shinto Unitarian Universalism Influence of religious views</cover>See also Adapted from Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Share this: Like this: Search Quote of the Day \" , \"segment\": \"Euthanasia may also be acceptable if it is used for selfless motives. On the other hand, by helping to end a life, even one filled with suffering, a person is disturbing the timing of the cycle of death and rebirth. This is a bad thing to do, and those involved in the euthanasia will take on the remaining karma of the patient Death is a natural process, and will come in time. It is clearly stated in the Vedas that man has only two trustworthy friends in life, the first is called Vidya (knowledge), and the 2nd is called Mrityu (Death). The former is something that is beneficial and a requirement in life, and the "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"29/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_489123501#5_1051298527","relevance":3,"reason":"This is a substantive segment of the dedicated 'Religious Views on Euthanasia' page. It directly provides Eastern/Hindu evidence, explaining that euthanasia may be acceptable for selfless motives but may also be opposed because it disturbs the cycle of death and rebirth and transfers the patient's remaining karma.","learned":"Hindu views can be ambivalent: euthanasia may be seen as a selfless act to end suffering, but also as interfering with death/rebirth and karma."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3211","reason":"The religious-views query continues to produce grade-3 segments with direct East-West comparative evidence, so continue retrieving further substantive sections such as Jainism, Islam, or Christianity."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3211"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_489123501#6_1051300352","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3211","rank":10,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_489123501#6_1051300352\" , \"end_char\": 5198.000000 , \"headings\": \"Religious Views on Euthanasia Religious Views on Euthanasia Page Contents Buddhism Christianity Catholicism Protestantism Christians in support of <cover>euthanasia Hinduism Islam Jainism Judaism Shinto Unitarian Universalism Influence of religious views</cover>See also Adapted from Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Share this: Like this: Search Quote of the Day \" , \"segment\": \"It is not the euthanasia that is the act of sin, but worldly attachment which causes euthanasia to be looked upon as an act of sin. Even a Sannyasin or Sannyasini if they decide to, are permitted to end his or her life with the hope of reaching moksha i.e. emancipation of the soul. Doctor harms a patient with cutting tube in the hospital Islam Muslims are against euthanasia. They believe that all humans life is sacred because it is given by God, and that God chooses how long each person lives. Human beings should not interfere in this. It is forbidden for a Muslim to plan, or come to know through self-will, "}}
