{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_489123501#6_1051300352","relevance":3,"reason":"This is a substantive segment of the dedicated 'Religious Views on Euthanasia' page. It directly provides Eastern/Hindu evidence about moksha and worldly attachment, then contrasts it with Islamic opposition based on the sacredness of God-given life, supporting variation across Eastern and Abrahamic traditions.","learned":"Some Hindu perspectives can permit life-ending by renunciants in hope of moksha, while Islamic views generally oppose euthanasia because life is sacred, given by God, and humans should not interfere with its divinely chosen length."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3211","reason":"The current query remains highly productive, yielding substantive sections from a dedicated comparative religious-views page; continue for more direct evidence such as Jainism, Judaism, or influence-of-religion sections."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3211"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_489123501#13_1051312848","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3211","rank":11,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_489123501#13_1051312848\" , \"end_char\": 10000.000000 , \"headings\": \"Religious Views on Euthanasia Religious Views on Euthanasia Page Contents Buddhism Christianity Catholicism Protestantism Christians in support of <cover>euthanasia Hinduism Islam Jainism Judaism Shinto Unitarian Universalism Influence of religious views</cover>See also Adapted from Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Share this: Like this: Search Quote of the Day \" , \"segment\": \"The findings concluded that the religious affiliation one associates with does not necessarily connect with their stance on euthanasia. Research shows that while many belong to a specific religion, they may not always see every aspect as relevant to them. Some metadata analysis has supported the hypothesis that nurses’ attitudes towards euthanasia and physician assisted suicide are influenced by religion and world view. Attributing more importance to religion also seems to make agreement with euthanasia and physician assisted suicide less likely. A 1995 study of public opinion found that the tendency to see a distinction between active euthanasia and suicide was clearly affected by religious affiliation and education. In Australia, more doctors without formal religious "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"31/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_489123501#13_1051312848","relevance":3,"reason":"This is a substantive segment of the dedicated 'Religious Views on Euthanasia' page. It directly discusses how religious affiliation, worldview, and religiosity influence attitudes toward euthanasia and physician-assisted suicide, supporting variation across East-West/religious perspectives.","learned":"Religious affiliation does not always determine a person's stance, but stronger importance placed on religion is associated with lower agreement with euthanasia or physician-assisted suicide; attitudes are also affected by education and worldview."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3211","reason":"The query is still producing grade-3 segments from a dedicated comparative religious-views document, so continue to collect remaining substantive evidence if available."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3211"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_489123501#12_1051310845","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3211","rank":12,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_489123501#12_1051310845\" , \"end_char\": 8913.000000 , \"headings\": \"Religious Views on Euthanasia Religious Views on Euthanasia Page Contents Buddhism Christianity Catholicism Protestantism Christians in support of <cover>euthanasia Hinduism Islam Jainism Judaism Shinto Unitarian Universalism Influence of religious views</cover>See also Adapted from Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Share this: Like this: Search Quote of the Day \" , \"segment\": \"In 1988 the UUA gathered to share a commitment to The Right to Die with Dignity document which included a resolution supporting self-determination in dying. Influence of religious views Religious views on euthanasia are both varied and complicated. While one’s view on the matter doesn’t necessarily connect directly to their religion, it often impacts a person’s opinion. While the influence of religion on one’s views toward palliative care do make a difference, they often play a smaller role than one may think. An analysis of the connection between the religion of US adults and their view on euthanasia was done in order to see how they combine. The findings concluded "}}
