{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_1099445187#14_1968700319","relevance":3,"reason":"This is a substantive segment of the Pew Research Center article dedicated to religious groups' views on end-of-life issues. It directly provides Eastern/Hindu evidence, explaining karma-based concerns about hastening death while noting a minority Hindu view that some severe circumstances could justify asking a doctor to hasten death, within a comparative article covering Eastern and Western traditions.","learned":"Hindu views often oppose euthanasia because stopping suffering may interfere with karma and rebirth, but some Hindus believe hastening death can be justified when illness prevents proper worship; Pew says most Hindus probably would not accept that exception."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3215","reason":"The Pew query has been consistently yielding grade-3 comparative religious-view evidence; continue only if further judgment budget is available to capture any remaining substantive sections."}}
